Quick Cheat Sheet for Domain 2

S3 STORAGE CLASSES:
├── Accessed daily          → Standard
├── Accessed < 1x/month     → Standard-IA
├── Unpredictable access     → Intelligent-Tiering
├── Archive (mins retrieval) → Glacier Instant/Flexible
└── Deep archive (hours)     → Glacier Deep Archive

REDSHIFT SORT KEYS:
├── One dominant filter column    → Compound
└── Multiple equal filter columns → Interleaved

REDSHIFT DISTRIBUTION:
├── Join column (high cardinality) → KEY
├── Small dimension table          → ALL
├── No clear join pattern          → EVEN
└── Skewed KEY? → Change to higher-cardinality column

LAKE FORMATION SECURITY:
├── Hide columns     → Column-level permissions
├── Hide rows        → Row-level security (data filters)
└── Scale governance → Tag-based access control (LF-Tags)

PARTITIONING vs BUCKETING:
├── Few distinct values + common filter → Partition
├── High cardinality + would cause explosion → Bucket
└── Already too many partitions → Partition projection

DATA LAKE UPSERTS/SCD:
└── Always → Apache Hudi or Delta Lake

TAG-BASED ACCESS CONTROL:
├── "Marketing team can access tables tagged 'dept=marketing'"
├── "Finance team can access tables tagged 'dept=finance'"
└── Controls ACCESS to entire resources (tables/columns)
    → It's a GOVERNANCE MODEL for organizing permissions at scale

ROW-LEVEL SECURITY (DATA FILTERS):
├── "Analyst X can see this table BUT only rows where region != 'EU'"
├── "Manager Y can see only rows where department = 'sales'"
└── Controls WHICH DATA within a table is visible
    → It's a DATA FILTER on query results

COMPOUND vs INTERLEAVED SORT KEY:
├── Compound: Filters MUST go left-to-right (like a phone book)
│   └── KEY(a, b, c): WHERE a=? ✅ | WHERE a=? AND b=? ✅ | WHERE b=? ❌
├── Interleaved: Any column combo works
│   └── KEY(a, b, c): WHERE b=? ✅ | WHERE c=? ✅ | WHERE a=? AND c=? ✅
└── Trade-off: Interleaved needs VACUUM REINDEX (higher maintenance)

PARTITIONING vs BUCKETING:
├── Low cardinality (< 1000 values) → PARTITION
├── High cardinality (millions of values) → BUCKET
└── No "index" concept in S3 data lakes!

LAKE FORMATION — COMBINE FEATURES:
├── Table/resource access at scale → Tag-based access
├── + Hide columns → Add column-level security
└── + Hide rows → Add row-level security (data filters)
    (They stack! One question can need multiple features)

KEY RULE:
├── COMPOUND sort key + new data loaded → VACUUM SORT ONLY
├── INTERLEAVED sort key + new data loaded → VACUUM REINDEX
├── Many deletes → VACUUM DELETE ONLY
├── ANALYZE → updates statistics only (doesn't fix sort order!)
└── ANALYZE helps query PLANNING, not query EXECUTION on unsorted data

SORT KEY vs DISTRIBUTION KEY:
├── Sort key → optimizes WHERE / range filters / ORDER BY
├── Distribution key → optimizes GROUP BY / JOIN (co-location)
├── Don't add GROUP BY columns to sort key (no benefit)
└── Keep sort key focused on filter columns only

HISTORICAL DATA — SNAPSHOTS vs TIME-TRAVEL:
├── Full daily snapshots = massive storage waste
├── Hudi/Iceberg time-travel = stores only deltas
├── Same functionality (query any past date)
├── 10-100x less storage
└── "Balance cost + functionality" = time-travel

GSI DESIGN:
├── Always include Sort Key if you need ordered results
├── GSI without SK = unordered results
├── PK = what you query BY; SK = how you SORT within results
└── Think: "What's my access pattern?" → PK filters, SK sorts

1. Redshift AQUA (Advanced Query Accelerator)

WHAT: Hardware-accelerated cache for Redshift Spectrum/S3 queries
HOW: Pushes filtering & aggregation to the storage layer (custom hardware)

KEY FACTS:
├── Accelerates queries that scan large amounts of S3 data
├── Works automatically (no query changes needed)
├── Available on RA3 node types only
├── Reduces data movement between storage and compute
├── Best for: scan-heavy queries with simple predicates (WHERE, LIKE, aggregations)
└── NOT helpful for: complex joins between local tables

EXAM TRIGGER:
├── "Accelerate Redshift Spectrum queries" → AQUA (if RA3 nodes)
├── "Reduce data movement in Redshift" → AQUA
├── "RA3 nodes + scan-heavy workloads" → AQUA helps automatically
└── Rarely tested — just know it exists and works with RA3
12. Redshift Workload Management (WLM) & Concurrency Scaling

WLM (Workload Management):
├── Queues: Separate workloads into priority queues
│   ├── Queue 1: "ETL" — 5 slots, 50% memory
│   ├── Queue 2: "Analysts" — 10 slots, 30% memory
│   └── Queue 3: "Dashboards" — 3 slots, 20% memory
├── Slots: Max concurrent queries per queue
├── Memory: % of cluster memory allocated per queue
├── Timeout: Kill queries exceeding time limit
└── Auto WLM: Redshift manages slots/memory automatically (RECOMMENDED)

CONCURRENCY SCALING:
├── Automatically adds temporary cluster capacity during spikes
├── Handles burst read queries (not writes)
├── Queries routed to scaling clusters transparently
├── 1 hour free per day per cluster (then pay per-second)
├── Enable per queue (not cluster-wide)
└── Use case: Dashboard refresh at 9 AM causes query spike

SHORT-DURATION QUERY ACCELERATION (SQA):
├── Prioritizes short queries over long-running ones
├── Short queries jump ahead in the queue
├── Prevents analysts waiting behind a 2-hour ETL query
└── Enable: SET max_execution_time for SQA threshold

EXAM TRIGGER:
├── "Separate ETL from analyst queries" → WLM queues
├── "Query spike during business hours" → Concurrency Scaling
├── "Short queries waiting behind long ETL" → SQA
├── "Least operational overhead for workload management" → Auto WLM
└── "Limit query runtime" → WLM timeout setting
WHAT: Managed Elasticsearch/OpenSearch for search & log analytics
WHEN TO USE (in data engineering context):

├── Full-text search across documents
├── Log analytics (CloudWatch Logs → OpenSearch → Kibana dashboards)
├── Real-time application monitoring
├── Clickstream analytics with real-time dashboards
└── Complement to data lake (search layer on top of S3 data)

COMMON PIPELINE:
Kinesis/Firehose → OpenSearch (real-time search/dashboards)
DynamoDB Streams → Lambda → OpenSearch (search on DynamoDB data)
S3 → Glue → OpenSearch (batch indexing for search)

OpenSearch vs OTHER SERVICES:
├── Need full-text search? → OpenSearch
├── Need SQL analytics on structured data? → Redshift/Athena
├── Need real-time log dashboards? → OpenSearch + Kibana
├── Need BI dashboards on structured data? → QuickSight + Redshift
└── Firehose destination? → ✅ Yes (direct delivery to OpenSearch)

EXAM TRIGGER:
├── "Search across log data" → OpenSearch
├── "Real-time dashboards for application logs" → OpenSearch + Kibana
├── "Full-text search on documents" → OpenSearch
├── "Firehose → search/analytics" → Firehose → OpenSearch
└── "Structured data analytics" → NOT OpenSearch (use Redshift/Athena)

14. Amazon Neptune & Keyspaces (When to Use)

AMAZON NEPTUNE (Graph Database):
├── Use for: Relationship-heavy data (social networks, fraud detection,
│            knowledge graphs, recommendation engines)
├── Query languages: Gremlin (traversal), SPARQL (RDF)
├── NOT for: Tabular analytics, time-series, simple key-value
└── Exam trigger: "Relationships between entities", "graph queries",
    "fraud ring detection", "social network analysis"

AMAZON KEYSPACES (Cassandra-compatible):
├── Use for: Existing Apache Cassandra workloads migrating to AWS
├── Serverless, pay-per-request
├── CQL (Cassandra Query Language) compatible
├── NOT for: New applications (use DynamoDB instead — better AWS integration)
└── Exam trigger: "Migrate Cassandra to AWS", "CQL compatible",
    "existing Cassandra application"

EXAM DECISION:
├── "Graph relationships" → Neptune
├── "Cassandra migration" → Keyspaces
├── "Key-value / document store" → DynamoDB
├── "Time-series data" → Amazon Timestream
├── "Relational + ACID" → RDS / Aurora
└── "Analytics on structured data" → Redshift
15. S3 Select / Glacier Select

WHAT: Query INSIDE an S3 object using SQL (without downloading entire object)
FORMAT: Works on CSV, JSON, Parquet objects

HOW IT WORKS:
├── Normal S3: Download entire 5 GB file → filter in your app
├── S3 Select: Send SQL → S3 filters server-side → returns only matching rows
├── Saves: bandwidth, time, compute cost
└── SQL subset: SELECT, WHERE, LIMIT (no JOIN, GROUP BY, subqueries)

EXAMPLE:
  SELECT s.name, s.age FROM S3Object s WHERE s.age > 30

GLACIER SELECT:
├── Same concept but for Glacier-stored objects
├── Query archived data without restoring entire object
├── Output goes to S3 (not returned directly)
└── Takes minutes to hours (not instant like S3 Select)

LIMITATIONS:
├── Single object only (cannot query across multiple files)
├── No JOINs, no aggregations (GROUP BY)
├── Max object size: 256 GB (CSV/JSON), 1 TB (Parquet)
├── For multi-file queries → use Athena instead
└── For complex SQL → use Athena instead

EXAM TRIGGER:
├── "Filter rows from a single large CSV in S3 without downloading" → S3 Select
├── "Reduce data transfer costs for simple filtering" → S3 Select
├── "Query across multiple S3 files" → Athena (NOT S3 Select)
├── "Complex aggregations on S3 data" → Athena (NOT S3 Select)
└── "Query archived data in Glacier" → Glacier Select

16. Data Lake Zones — Design Principles

THREE-ZONE ARCHITECTURE:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                             │
│  RAW ZONE (Landing/Bronze)                                  │
│  ├── Original data as-is (JSON, CSV, logs)                 │
│  ├── Immutable (never modify source data)                  │
│  ├── Partitioned by ingestion date                         │
│  ├── Storage: S3 Standard → lifecycle to IA/Glacier        │
│  └── Purpose: Audit trail, reprocessing capability         │
│                          ↓ ETL (Glue/EMR)                  │
│                                                             │
│  CURATED ZONE (Cleaned/Silver)                             │
│  ├── Cleaned, validated, deduplicated                      │
│  ├── Columnar format (Parquet/ORC)                         │
│  ├── Schema enforced (Glue Catalog)                        │
│  ├── Partitioned by business keys (date, region)           │
│  ├── Storage: S3 Standard                                  │
│  └── Purpose: Source of truth for analytics                │
│                          ↓ Aggregation                     │
│                                                             │
│  AGGREGATED ZONE (Consumption/Gold)                        │
│  ├── Pre-computed summaries, KPIs, materialized views      │
│  ├── Optimized for specific use cases (dashboards, ML)     │
│  ├── May be in Redshift, Athena views, or S3              │
│  ├── Storage: S3 Standard (frequently accessed)            │
│  └── Purpose: Fast consumption by analysts/dashboards      │
│                                                             │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

KEY PRINCIPLES:
├── Raw is IMMUTABLE — never modify, always append
├── Each zone has progressively higher data quality
├── Governance (Lake Formation) applies at all zones
├── Catalog (Glue) tracks metadata for all zones
└── Access control: Raw (data engineers only), Curated (analysts), Aggregated (everyone)

EXAM TRIGGER:
├── "Where to store original unmodified data" → Raw zone
├── "Source of truth for analytics" → Curated zone
├── "Pre-computed dashboards" → Aggregated zone
├── "Data quality improves as data moves through zones" → Bronze → Silver → Gold
└── "Immutable audit trail" → Raw zone (never modify)
